How to prevent Transaction ID wraparound in PostgreSQL?

PostgreSQL gives every transaction a number (called transaction ID or XID).
These numbers keep increasing — and when they reach their limit (about 2 billion),
they wrap around (start from 0 again).

If old data isn’t cleaned (or “frozen”) before that, Postgres can’t tell which rows are old or new, so it stops
accepting writes to prevent corruption.
