⚡ Java Performance Tuning – Real World Scenario

🔹 Scenario – Memory Leak in a Spring Boot Service

A payments microservice was running smoothly in production but started crashing every 6–8 hours with OutOfMemoryError.
Monitoring showed the heap usage kept growing without dropping, even after multiple GC cycles.

👉 Root Cause
Heap dump analysis revealed a large number of HashMap objects retained in memory. A static cache was holding references to session objects and never releasing them. This caused the JVM heap to grow until it eventually ran out of space.

👉 Fix

Replaced the static cache with a proper LRU cache (Caffeine/Guava).

Configured eviction policies (time + size based), e.g.:


Cache<String, Session> cache = Caffeine.newBuilder()
 .maximumSize(1000) // evict after 1000 entries
 .expireAfterWrite(30, TimeUnit.MINUTES) // evict 30 mins after write
 .build();

Added monitoring with Micrometer + Prometheus to track cache hit/miss and memory usage.


After the fix, memory stabilized, GC cycles normalized, and the service ran continuously without crashes.

👉 Debugging Flow (Heap Dump)
Service crash → Captured heap dump (jcmd / jmap) → Opened in Eclipse MAT → Found uncollected static references → Fixed root cause.

Note - Takeaway: Real-world tuning is about finding the root cause with the right tools (GC logs, heap dumps, profilers) and then applying targeted fixes — not random JVM flag tweaking.

