My Spring Boot application kept crashing in production, and for a week, I had no idea why. 🤯 The logs were clean, and everything worked perfectly on my local machine. It felt like I was chasing a ghost.


It turned out to be a classic memory leak. A simple caching mechanism I built was holding onto objects forever,
never letting the Java Garbage Collector (GC) do its job. The heap just grew and grew until the application ran out of memory and died.
It was a humbling reminder that while the JVM is powerful, you still need to understand what is happening under the hood to prevent an OutOfMemoryError.

My breakthrough came when I fired up VisualVM, a Java profiler. It gave me a visual map of the memory heap,
and I could see exactly which objects were piling up. The fix was simple in the end, I switched to a proper cache implementation like Caffeine that has built in eviction policies.
Lesson learned: always be mindful of object scopes and lifecycles, especially when dealing with static collections or other long lived objects. 🛠️



This experience went beyond just fixing a bug. It taught me a crucial lesson about system design for microservices. In a world of Docker containers and Kubernetes orchestration,
 efficient memory management is non negotiable. A leaky service does not just crash, it eats up resources, costs money, and can bring down other services in the cluster. Setting up proper monitoring and profiling as part of your DevOps CI/CD pipeline is just as important as writing unit tests. 🚀



